# HSM collector conformance v1 — large file values: the piece protocol on the file connection of the collector session
# (#1888 managed, #1887 native; ADR-0022; wire contract "File connection").
#
# The collector is on a session to the driver's SCRIPTED SESSION SERVER (create_collector_on_session), which also takes
# the file connection: the upgrade with hsm.files.v1 and X-Hsm-Session, the hello with fileLimits (pieceBytes 65536 unless
# file_server_limits says otherwise), the probe, `begin`, binary piece frames, `end` and `discard`, one text ack each.
# Without a script the server answers as the real one does: a part per file (sensor path, name, size, modified, source),
# pieces checked by CRC-32, zstd frame and length, `end` checked against the SHA-256 of the piece digests, `stored` for a
# file it stored before. A file of 200000 bytes is four pieces: 0, 1, 2 (65536 each) and 3 (3392).
#
# Files are written by the driver into the case's own folder, with content both drivers reproduce from a seed: xorshift32
# (x ^= x << 13; x ^= x >> 17; x ^= x << 5 on uint32; state = seed, 1 when the seed is 0), one byte per step, the low
# byte of the new state; modified 2026-11-25T10:00:00Z unless the verb names another time. Each case has its own data
# folder, so large_file_restart finds that case's state only.
#
# Time is the case's, never the wall clock's: the uploader's clock moves only when a verb moves it (a second at a time,
# a scan of the watched folders after every second) and when the uploader waits a server's retryAfter, which passes at
# once. The expect_* verbs that wait move the clock a second per poll, so a wait the uploader set (a retry, a file put
# back) passes however late it was set. A request the script leaves unanswered ends its ack wait when the server decides
# so (the driver's ack-deadline seam), not by the request timeout.
#
# The scripted server offers file connections only after file_server_accept_upgrades (without it, it is a server older
# than large files, as in every other fixture): each case here says so after create_collector_on_session.
#
# Both collectors implement it: the managed collector since 4.2.0 (#1888), the native collector since 0.16.0 (#1887). The
# native driver runs it in builds with large files (HSM_COLLECTOR_HTTP and HSM_COLLECTOR_TABLES).
#
# Verbs (sources and calls):
# large_file_create|name|size|seed[|modified]      writes a source file into the case's folder
# large_file_send|sensor|name|keep_or_delete        hands the file over (managed SendLargeFileAsync)
# large_file_watch|sensor|mask|settle_s|keep_or_delete  watches the case's folder (managed WatchLargeFiles)
# large_file_send_not_secure|sensor|name|keep_or_delete  hands the file over with the option Secure off
# large_file_send_without_alert|sensor|name|keep_or_delete  hands the file over with the option NewFileAlert off
# large_file_change_after_piece|name|piece          once piece `piece` of the file was answered, the file's modification
#                                                   time is an hour later (a file that changed while it was sent; the
#                                                   driver may change what the collector reads, since Windows denies
#                                                   other writers while the collector holds the file)
# large_file_clock_advance|seconds                  the case's clock, a second at a time, a scan after each
# large_file_restart                                stop and dispose the collector; a new one on the same server and data folder
# create_collector_on_http                          a collector on the HTTP transport (nothing listens): large files unavailable
# Verbs (the server's script):
# file_server_limits|piece_bytes|max_file_bytes     what the file hello states from now on
# file_server_refuse_upgrades|status / file_server_accept_upgrades
# file_server_holds|name|pieces                     the server already has the first `pieces` pieces of that file
# file_server_answer_begin_next|answer              stored, checking (202, retryAfter 1), no_room (503, retryAfter 1),
#                                                   busy (409, retryAfter 1), too_large (413), cannot_take (422), invalid (400),
#                                                   drop, silent, close:<code>
# file_server_answer_piece_next|answer              real (the server's own answer), checksum_mismatch[:next],
#                                                   out_of_order:<next>, fault (503, retryAfter 1), discarded (400), drop,
#                                                   drop_after_store (stored, then the connection cut), silent, close:<code>
# file_server_answer_end_next|answer                checking, mismatch (400: discarded), missing:<next>, cannot_take, drop,
#                                                   drop_after_store, silent, close:<code>
# Verbs (expectations):
# expect_large_file_result|name|outcome|timeout_s   stored, refused, given_up, missing, unavailable, cancelled
# expect_large_file_pending|name                    the hand-over has not settled
# expect_large_file_watch_state|index|state         active, unavailable, refused, disposed (index 0 = the first watch)
# expect_large_file_source|name|present_or_absent
# expect_large_files_not_sent|count                 refused, given up or missing, over the case's hand-overs and watches
# expect_file_stored|name|seed|timeout_s            stored and checked by the server; its bytes are the seeded content
# expect_file_stored_count|name|count
# expect_file_begins|name|count|timeout_s           begins of the file the server received
# expect_routed_file_values_waiting|count|timeout_s file values above a quarter of the frame that the route took and that
#                                                   wait for the file connection (not yet stored, given back or dropped)
# expect_file_begin_order|name,name,...             the first begin of each file, in order
# expect_file_begin_field|index|field|value         a field of begin number `index` (0 = first), as its JSON text
# expect_file_begin_field_starts|index|field|prefix the same field starts with the prefix
# expect_file_begin_field_absent|index|field        begin number `index` does not carry the field
# expect_file_stored_size|name|bytes|timeout_s      stored and checked by the server, with this many bytes
# expect_file_pieces|n0,n1,...                      the piece number of every piece frame received, in order
# expect_file_discards|count|timeout_s              `discard` requests the server took
# expect_file_connections_at_least|count|timeout_s
# expect_refused_file_upgrades_at_least|count|timeout_s

